---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageFrame from '../components/PageFrame.astro';
import Masthead from '../components/Masthead.astro';
import WorkSection from '../components/WorkSection.astro';
import ActionRow from '../components/ActionRow.astro';
import TextLink from '../components/TextLink.astro';
import Dot from '../components/Dot.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import FactsList from '../components/FactsList.astro';
import CaseLead from '../components/CaseLead.astro';
import MetaLine from '../components/MetaLine.astro';
import PointsList from '../components/PointsList.astro';
const head = `<link rel="icon" href="data:," />`;
const afterFooter = ``;
---
<Base title={"Anima - Gorka Molero Case Study"} description={"Anima is a system I built for creating open-world point-and-click games with AI. Shadow Over Innsmouth, a Lovecraft adventure, is the game I'm making with it."} page="anima">
  <Fragment slot="head" set:html={head} />
  <PageFrame class="sheet">
    <Masthead />
    

      <CaseLead class="lead">
        <MetaLine class="meta eyebrow">Case · open-world point-and-click</MetaLine>
        <h2 class="case-title">Anima</h2>
        <p class="intro">Anima is a system I built for creating open-world point-and-click games with AI. You write the story, and AI makes the characters, the scenes and whatever happens when a player tries something the story doesn't cover. Shadow Over Innsmouth, a Lovecraft adventure, is the game I'm making with it, and it isn't finished yet.</p>
        <MetaLine class="meta chips">Game creation with AI <Dot class="dot">&middot;</Dot> Phaser and React <Dot class="dot">&middot;</Dot> Playable prototype</MetaLine>
      </CaseLead>

      <main>
      <WorkSection class="work" id="playing">
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><video controls playsinline preload="none" poster="./assets/creative/point-and-click-bar-poster.jpg" src="https://pub-066596d7e4fe4de2b74eac13bb03fe55.r2.dev/point-and-click-bar.mp4" aria-label="An earlier point-and-click game of mine, which Anima grew out of: a bar scene where the player picks a line or types anything, and the characters answer."></video></div>
          <figcaption>An earlier point-and-click game of mine, which Anima grew out of. In the bar you can pick a line or type anything you like, and each character answers in their own voice.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work">
        <h2>Problem</h2>
        <p>A point-and-click adventure normally needs a writer, artists, animators and programmers. In an open world the player can click anything, so nobody can write every outcome by hand.</p>
        <p>In Anima's open-world mode you can click any object in Innsmouth. A narrator offers three things to do, or you type your own. If you type that you set the van on fire, the van in the picture burns. When you go somewhere new, the place is generated, your character can walk around it, and it's still there the next time you come back. The narrator is the story guide from <a class="inline-link" href="./the-pulse.html">The Pulse</a>, my voice-narrated interactive fiction. The open world is a playable prototype so far.</p>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work">
        <h2>What I Built</h2>
        <p>Anima is a game engine, an editor and an art pipeline that all run from the same story script. The engine runs on Phaser, the editor is built in React, and each player's world is saved in a database behind a Hono API.</p>
        <FactsList class="facts">
          <li><strong>Language</strong><span>StoryScript, my own language for writing the story and everything you can click.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Engine</strong><span>Characters walk on the floor at the right size and step behind furniture. In the open world, new places are generated as you go.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Art pipeline</strong><span>Walk cycles generated as video, cut out with a difference matte and turned into game sprites.</span></li>
        </FactsList>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work">
        <h2>Built on StoryScript</h2>
        <p>StoryScript is a plain-text language I designed for point-and-click adventures. You write the scenes, the dialogue, the things you can click and the inventory much like a screenplay. A language model can write it and a person can read it, and the engine runs it directly with no compile step. It also runs without graphics, so a story can be tested before any art exists, and an AI playtester can play through it. The Shadow Over Innsmouth script is about 6,500 lines across four acts. StoryScript has <a class="inline-link" href="./storyscript.html">its own case study</a>.</p>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work">
        <h2>The Game and the Editor</h2>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><video src="https://pub-066596d7e4fe4de2b74eac13bb03fe55.r2.dev/anima-cutscene.webm" poster="./assets/anima-cutscene-poster.webp" controls muted loop playsinline preload="none" width="1280" aria-label="Animated cutscene from Shadow Over Innsmouth: two characters talking in the Innsmouth hotel lobby at dusk."></video></div>
          <figcaption>A cutscene from Shadow Over Innsmouth, with Kat and Hermes in the hotel lobby at dusk. The scene, the lighting and the character animation were all made with Anima.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><img src="./assets/anima-depot-interior.webp" alt="Anima editor showing the Arkham Bus Depot interior scene: two characters, a departures board, and the Scene Review panel that lets you switch and inspect scenes." loading="lazy" /></div>
          <figcaption>The Arkham Bus Depot interior, open in the Anima editor. The Scene Review panel switches scenes, turns the clickable areas on and off, and shows what the engine is doing while the game runs.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work">
        <h2>The Art Pipeline</h2>
        <p>Each character starts as a walk cycle generated as video with Veo 3.1.</p>
        <Figure class="plate narrow">
          <div class="shot"><video src="https://pub-066596d7e4fe4de2b74eac13bb03fe55.r2.dev/anima-skyler-source.webm" poster="./assets/anima-skyler-source-poster.webp" controls muted loop playsinline preload="none" width="480" aria-label="Raw generated walk-cycle source video of Skyler, a rubberhose-style cartoon character, before pipeline processing."></video></div>
          <figcaption>A raw walk cycle for Skyler, straight from the video model before any processing.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><video src="https://pub-066596d7e4fe4de2b74eac13bb03fe55.r2.dev/anima-sprite-pipeline.webm" poster="./assets/anima-sprite-pipeline-poster.webp" controls muted loop playsinline preload="none" width="1080" aria-label="Three-way comparison of a walk-cycle sprite (Ashley) processed through different pipeline variants and cut out with a difference matte for spritesheet extraction."></video></div>
          <figcaption>Ashley's walk cycle run through three versions of the pipeline, side by side, over magenta and blue so stray pixels stand out. Comparing them is how I check that the sprites stay clean and consistent before they're cut into a spritesheet.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><img src="./assets/anima-pipeline.svg" alt="Diagram of Anima: authoring, dialogue logic, editor tools, asset pipeline, API and data, runtime and playable output." loading="lazy" /></div>
          <figcaption>The story and dialogue feed the editor, the art pipeline makes the spritesheets, and the engine puts both together into a scene you can play.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <h3>How the cutout works</h3>
        <p>The video model puts the character on a flat grey background. Standard background removal leaves halos around the character and mistakes the shadows on skin for background. So I use a difference matte. The frames are packed into grids, and an image model, Nano Banana, redraws each grid twice, once on pure white and once on pure black. A pixel that looks the same in both versions belongs to the character. A pixel that changes is background, so it becomes transparent. Comparing the two versions gives a clean edge, even on skin.</p>
        <p>The default video decoder drops the transparency, so I decode with the VP9 alpha decoder. Then I find the frame where the walk loops cleanly, crop every frame to the same box so the character doesn't jitter, and pack the frames into a spritesheet the engine loads.</p>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work">
        <h2>Technical Decisions</h2>
        <PointsList class="points">
          <li>In the open world, most clicks only change the narration, the voice and the character's animation. A new image is made only when something visible changes. Every generated place is cached and shared, so a place someone has already visited loads straight away.</li>
          <li>Language models are bad at pixel positions, so the floor and the perspective come from perception models. SAM 3 finds the floor, a depth model finds the horizon, and a vision model only answers whether the character looks right standing in the scene.</li>
          <li>The character's size comes from the depth of the scene. Every step is planned onto the floor so the feet don't slide, and the character can walk behind furniture and props.</li>
          <li>I test the walking without opening the game in a browser. A test harness replays 139 walks across ten scenes and checks every frame.</li>
        </PointsList>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work">
        <h2>Demo</h2>
        <p>The demo covers the open world, the editor and the parts of Shadow Over Innsmouth built so far. The unreleased story text and the generated voices stay private.</p>
        <ActionRow class="actions"><TextLink class="button primary" href="./contact.html">Ask for a demo</TextLink></ActionRow>
      </WorkSection>
      </main>
  </PageFrame>
  <Fragment slot="after-footer" set:html={afterFooter} />
</Base>
